# EPH-Multidimensional-Poverty-XAI

Shiny App: https://lm-hcor.shinyapps.io/EPH-Pobreza-multidimensional-XAI/

## Analysis of the Determinants of Multidimensional Poverty in Argentina: A Regional and Intertemporal Perspective Using Explainable Artificial Intelligence (2016-2025)

This repository contains the source code, data architecture, and analytical pipeline developed for my Master’s Thesis.

The study moves beyond traditional one-dimensional poverty measurements based exclusively on income by proposing a hybrid approach combining Machine Learning models and advanced Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) techniques.

The objective is to open the "black box" of predictive models and identify the structural determinants of social vulnerability in Argentina at the federal level.

---

# 🛠️ Methodology and Statistical Rigor

To ensure that the model could be used as a simulation and decision-support tool for public policy analysis, the data ecosystem was designed under strict macroeconomic and statistical controls.

## Regional and Intertemporal Income Deflation

Nominal income flows (`itcf_real`, `p21_real`) were adjusted to constant prices using the Consumer Price Index (CPI) disaggregated across Argentina’s six major statistical regions.

This approach reduces the bias introduced by national-level inflation averages in contexts of high regional inflation heterogeneity.

## Multidimensional Poverty Index (MPI)

A binary target variable (`mpi_pobre`) was constructed by adapting the Alkire-Foster methodology.

The index integrates multiple deprivation dimensions:

- Housing quality
- Basic sanitation
- Healthcare access (`priv_salud`)
- Education

## Post-Census Demographic Alignment

Regional recalibration of sampling weights (`pondera`) was performed to approximate the demographic structure of the 2022 national census framework, reducing potential data drift between training and validation datasets.

---

# Prevention of Geographic and Temporal Data Leakage

## Block Cross-Validation

Urban clusters (`grupo_cv`) were grouped using `group_vfold_cv`.

All observations belonging to the same urban agglomeration were strictly assigned either to the training or validation dataset, preserving spatial independence assumptions.

## Chronological Time Splitting

A strict separation was implemented between historical data (2016-2024, based on the 2010 Census framework) and the external validation dataset (2025, based on the 2022 Census framework).

This approach allows the evaluation of model robustness under institutional and sampling framework changes.

---

# 🤖 Modeling Pipeline and Architecture Optimization

The analytical pipeline was optimized using the `tidymodels` ecosystem to operate efficiently under strict hardware limitations (16GB RAM in Windows 11 environments), reducing total computational time to less than 16 hours.

Pipeline:
[Original Data]
↓
[Imputation / Dummy Encoding]
↓
[External SMOTE-NC]
↓
[MCA Dimensionality Reduction]
↓
[ANOVA Racing Cross-Validation]


---

# 1. Class Imbalance Correction (External SMOTE-NC)

Due to the minority nature of extreme multidimensional poverty observations, SMOTE-NC (`themis`) was applied using an `over_ratio = 0.25`.

### Critical optimization:

To avoid the computational bottleneck caused by repeatedly calculating nearest-neighbor distances inside each cross-validation fold, SMOTE-NC was executed once externally on the preprocessed training matrix.

Technical variables (`codusu`, `pondera`, `grupo_cv`) were synthetically reassigned through stratified sampling to preserve regional consistency.

---

# 2. Dimensionality Reduction (Multiple Correspondence Analysis)

A dynamic Multiple Correspondence Analysis (MCA) approach was implemented using `FactoMineR`.

The resulting latent coordinates (`mca_dim1`, `mca_dim2`) replaced highly correlated infrastructure variables, reducing multicollinearity and preventing artificial inflation of performance metrics.

Variables introducing direct data leakage with the Multidimensional Poverty Index were excluded.

---

# 3. Hyperparameter Optimization Through Racing Methods (ANOVA Racing)

Instead of exhaustive grid search, hyperparameter tuning was performed using ANOVA Race Tuning (`tune_race_anova` from the `finetune` package).

The algorithm evaluates parameter configurations through restricted parallel processing at the resampling level (`parallel_over = "resamples"`) using 2 PSOCK workers.

Inferior configurations are eliminated early after a burn-in phase of two folds, optimizing the Area Under the ROC Curve (AUC-ROC).

---

# 📈 Operational Performance Evaluation

Models were evaluated using threshold-independent metrics and calibrated reliability curves.

Classification thresholds were optimized according to the F1-score, prioritizing the optimal balance between:

- Precision (reducing incorrect fiscal targeting)
- Recall (maximizing social coverage)

| Model | Engine | Calibrated Threshold (t) | AUC-ROC Test 2024 | AUC-ROC External 2025 | Probability Calibration |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| CART | rpart | 0.404 | 0.711 | 0.690 | Poor / Stepwise |
| Random Forest | ranger | 0.430 | 0.863 | 0.838 | Systematic overestimation bias |
| XGBoost | xgboost | 0.444 | 0.865 | 0.841 | Excellent / Near-perfect alignment |

### Impact of the 2025 Census Framework Change

The model detects a controlled structural performance degradation (`Δ ≈ 0.024 AUC`) in the external 2025 dataset, demonstrating the validity of chronological splitting for evaluating model resilience under institutional changes.

### Calibration

XGBoost was selected as the final model for social resource allocation simulations because its predicted probabilities closely match observed empirical frequencies, allowing outputs to be interpreted as household-level poverty risk probabilities.

